  Play a Rogue: start with a stack of daggers and get class bonus of +1
to number of thrown daggers.   Throw early, throw often.  Later you can
dual-wield +Grayswandir and a +7 silver saber, and you should *still* be
throwing daggers early and often.  

  Ask Shabby^ on #nethack.  He played Rogues for a long time but *hated*
throwing daggers (Shabby^'s word).  Then he tried it, and Ascended.
Then he insisted his next role be able to get to Expert in daggers.)

  Turn on autoquiver.  You might adjust your dagger stack to 'Q' to make
manual requivering quick and sure.  Use the 'f' command to fire.  

  Daggers:
   - Multi-fire at higher skill levels.
   - Stack (can bless, repair, enchant whole stack at once.)
   - Don't disappear.
   - Can also be used as a wielded weapon. (Just one skill to enhance.)
   - Can be thrown at targets at melee range as well as farther.
   - Get strength damage bonus added to each dagger hit (thrown or
       wielded.)
   - Don't require a launcher.
   - You'll find plenty in the dungeon
   - +2 to-hit bonus makes them the easiest weapon to enhance starting
        at unskilled.

  Rangers, Rogues, Tourists, Valkyries, and Wizards can get to Expert in
dagger.   d3 daggers thrown per 'f'iring.  Average 2 daggers per throw.

  That's essentially two-weaponing daggers, for you two-weaponing fans
out there.  The main differences are: 

    1) You can still wear a shield.  
    2) It works at range.  
    3) You can wield any weapon you like.

  Oh, yeah, Elves throwing elven daggers and Rogues in general get a +1
bonus to number of daggers thrown.  They're 2.5 weaponing at Expert
skill.    (Interesting fact: Elven rangers using expert elven bow/arrows
fire d5 arrows per turn, three-weapon!.)

  Beats the hell out of running up to hit a monster.




